        title: "Prospect Theory & Cognitive Investor Biases",
        say: [
  "The cornerstone theoretical framework of behavioral finance is Prospect Theory, developed by Daniel Kahneman and Amos Tversky in 1979 (for which Kahneman won the 2002 Nobel Memorial Prize in Economic Sciences). Neoclassical economics assumed that investors maximize expected utility defined over total absolute wealth, displaying consistent risk aversion across all domains. Kahneman and Tversky proved experimentally that humans do not evaluate decisions based on final total wealth. Instead, they evaluate outcomes as Gains and Losses relative to an arbitrary, subjective Reference Point (typically the original purchase price of an investment). Prospect Theory is defined by three fundamental psychological features: 1. Reference Dependence: Wealth changes are evaluated as deviations from a mental reference point rather than absolute net worth. 2. Diminishing Sensitivity: The marginal psychological impact of both gains and losses decreases as the magnitude expands (represented by an S-shaped value function that is concave for gains and convex for losses). 3. Loss Aversion: The psychological pain of enduring a financial loss is approximately 2 to 2.5 times more intense than the pleasure experienced from an equivalent monetary gain: $$v(x) = \\begin{cases} x^\\alpha & \\text{for } x \\ge 0 \\\\ -\\lambda (-x)^\\alpha & \\text{for } x < 0 \\end{cases}$$ where empirical calibrations establish $\\alpha \\approx 0.88$ and the loss aversion coefficient $\\lambda \\approx 2.25$. (Step 2: Record secondary interest & fee accrual adjustment ($25,550).)",
  "Examine our Python execution above: - Gaining $\\$1,000$ delivers $+436.52$ units of psychological utility. - Losing $\\$1,000$ inflicts a punishing $-982.16$ units of negative utility! Because the loss-to-gain pain ratio is exactly $2.25\\times$, losing $\\$10,000$ inflicts more than double the emotional suffering that winning $\\$10,000$ creates in pleasure. Loss aversion drives several pervasive cognitive biases that distort financial market behavior: - The Disposition Effect: Investors routinely sell winning stocks too quickly to lock in mental gains (risk-averse in the domain of gains), while holding onto losing stocks far too long in the desperate hope that they will recover back to the break-even purchase price (risk-seeking in the domain of losses).",
  "This irrational behavior leads to poor tax-loss harvesting and inferior portfolio returns. - Mental Accounting: Formulated by Richard Thaler, investors segregate money into separate mental buckets based on origin (e.g., treating 'house money' from trading profits as less valuable than hard-earned salary, gambling recklessly with the former). - Anchoring and Adjustment: Investors anchor on arbitrary numbers (such as an all-time high stock price or initial purchase price) and fail to update their valuations when fundamental business realities deteriorate. - Overconfidence and Self-Attribution Bias: Investors attribute profitable trades to their own genius while blaming losses on external bad luck or market manipulation, leading to excessive trading volume and underperformance. Take a moment to compare how a novice approaches Prospect Theory & Cognitive Investor Biases versus how a seasoned specialist handles it. A novice looks only at the final output number, whereas a specialist inspects how Behavioral interacts with Market, checks whether Corporate remains within expected operating bounds, and verifies the fallback behavior when calculate_prospect_theory_utility hits an extreme edge case. That habit of checking both the happy path and the boundary condition is what turns textbook knowledge into reliable professional judgment.",
  "Behavioral finance integrates cognitive psychology with neoclassical financial theory, demonstrating how heuristics—such as overconfidence, loss aversion, anchoring, mental accounting, and herding—generate empirical asset pricing One common pitfall when implementing Prospect Theory & Cognitive Investor Biases is treating Market as a static constant rather than a dynamic input that must be re-verified as conditions evolve. Whenever Corporate or calculate_prospect_theory_utility changes, teams should immediately re-run their baseline checks on alpha to confirm that the original decision rule still holds. Building this verification step directly into your workflow eliminates guesswork and keeps your analysis rock-solid. Take a moment to compare how a novice approaches Prospect Theory & Cognitive Investor Biases versus how a seasoned specialist handles it. A novice looks only at the final output number, whereas a specialist inspects how Biases interacts with Behavioral, checks whether Market remains within expected operating bounds, and verifies the fallback behavior when Corporate hits an extreme edge case. That habit of checking both the happy path and the boundary condition is what turns textbook knowledge into reliable professional judgment."
],
        example: "def calculate_prospect_theory_utility(gain_loss_amount, alpha=0.88, lambda_loss_aversion=2.25):\n    # Tversky & Kahneman (1992) S-shaped value function:\n    # v(x) = x^alpha for x >= 0\n    # v(x) = -lambda * (-x)^alpha for x < 0\n    if gain_loss_amount >= 0:\n        utility = (gain_loss_amount ** alpha)\n    else:\n        utility = -lambda_loss_aversion * ((-gain_loss_amount) ** alpha)\n    return utility\n\ndollar_shifts = [1000.0, 5000.0, 10000.0]\n\nprint(f\"Kahneman-Tversky Prospect Theory Utility Analysis (Loss Aversion lambda = 2.25):\")\nprint(f\"{'Dollar Shift ($)':<18} | {'Utility of Gain (+x)':<24} | {'Utility of Loss (-x)':<24} | {'Loss / Gain Ratio':<18}\")\nprint(\"-\" * 88)\nfor d in dollar_shifts:\n    u_gain = calculate_prospect_theory_utility(d)\n    u_loss = calculate_prospect_theory_utility(-d)\n    ratio = abs(u_loss) / u_gain\n    print(f\"${d:>14,.0f}    | {u_gain:>20.2f}     | {u_loss:>20.2f}     | {ratio:>15.2f}x Pain\")\n",
        code: "def calculate_prospect_theory_utility(gain_loss_amount, alpha=0.88, lambda_loss_aversion=2.25):\n    # Tversky & Kahneman (1992) S-shaped value function:\n    # v(x) = x^alpha for x >= 0\n    # v(x) = -lambda * (-x)^alpha for x < 0\n    if gain_loss_amount >= 0:\n        utility = (gain_loss_amount ** alpha)\n    else:\n        utility = -lambda_loss_aversion * ((-gain_loss_amount) ** alpha)\n    return utility\n\ndollar_shifts = [1000.0, 5000.0, 10000.0]\n\nprint(f\"Kahneman-Tversky Prospect Theory Utility Analysis (Loss Aversion lambda = 2.25):\")\nprint(f\"{'Dollar Shift ($)':<18} | {'Utility of Gain (+x)':<24} | {'Utility of Loss (-x)':<24} | {'Loss / Gain Ratio':<18}\")\nprint(\"-\" * 88)\nfor d in dollar_shifts:\n    u_gain = calculate_prospect_theory_utility(d)\n    u_loss = calculate_prospect_theory_utility(-d)\n    ratio = abs(u_loss) / u_gain\n    print(f\"${d:>14,.0f}    | {u_gain:>20.2f}     | {u_loss:>20.2f}     | {ratio:>15.2f}x Pain\")\n",
